A||||6||AkursedX||86 Coupe.  Lots of ?'s||||1||
Z||000000||AkursedX||05-14-2001||11:25 PM||||Well, it looks like I will be joining the multiple-Fiero owner ranks very soon   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  <P>I found a 1986 coupe for 1k.  Specs:  72k, auto, gold w/tan interior, power windows, cruise. <BR>Well mechanically it sounds great!  The best sounding duke I've come across, and the engine compartment is clean, and it looks real good. Seats are flawless, but the radio pos had a little warping. <BR>Problems are:  Low idle, Engine has lots of shaking under acceleration, Headlights pop up, but are intermittent going down.  Tranny does a little "shudder"  just before coming to a complete stop.  E-Brake cables are shot.  The Blue dingy thingy likes to chime at random   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>So, my questions are:<BR>For 1000, is this a pretty good deal?  <P>I have my 88GT rims sitting around collecting dust, would these fit on it?<P>I know the dog bone is shot on the car, so I need to replace that, but should I replace the other mounts as well?  <P>How hard is it to replace the e-brake cables?<P>If the headlights are coming up fine, but going down intermittently, is this a sign of the motors going?<P>The "shudder" that I get, I think it's a bad Torque converter lockup solenoid, is that a pretty good guess?<P>I have used the search function as well, but I figured I would ask anyways to see what info i could get.<P>Edit:  needed to add another question  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by AkursedX (edited 05-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||05-15-2001||07:54 AM||||Hey X!<BR>You could use all those problems to chew down the price...<BR>I would offer $500, as it will take quite a bit to repair the defects...<BR>Espcially the e-brake cables!<BR>PITA!!!<BR>Otherwise pay the guy and take yer chances...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ken Wittlief||05-15-2001||10:13 AM||||I just replaced the Ebrake cables on my 85SE, the two rear ones, not the center one that goes to the handle.<P>  they werent that bad really. there is a spring clip on the caliper part that is around the end of the cable. Its not obvious until you look at the new one but you can pry the whole clip off sideways, instead of trying to push all those spreader things backwards though the hole.<P>  the harder part is where they go through the frame - the spring clips there are inside the frame there( there is an opening on one side but they are hard to get at). Mine were completely buried and packed in rust dust and mud, so I more or less ripped the cable from the frame with a big pair of vice grips. it worked for me, but I can see if the frame was really rusty there you might rip the hole open, then the new cable would have nothing to snap into.<P>  Maybe the center cable is the one thats a PITA. The other potential problem is if the ebrakes havent been used for a long time the arms on the calipers might be frozen up - if you have to replace calipers to get the ebrakes working they are about $70 for each side.<P>  As a general rule, if the car is not running like you want it to, you have to estimate the most it might cost and the least it might cost to 'make it right'.<P>  and then decide how much you are willing to pay, knowing if the low estimate is what it takes, you will have gotten a good deal.<P>  but you still want to be happy with the car, even if your high estimate of repairs ends up being your end cost.<P>  when you buy a used car thats not running right, unless you are absolutely sure you know whats wrong with it, you are always taking a gamble.<P>  but thats life, right? Sometimes you get the bear, and sometimes the bear gets you.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Ken Wittlief||05-15-2001||10:18 AM||||Oh hey I just noticed you are in buffalo (area code theif!)<P>I was gonna say a car with 77k miles, with a great interior for $1k is a good potential project car, but you also need to crawl around underneight it to see how much rust is on the space frame and suspension.<P>Dont listen to what the owner says about 'no winters!' Look in the wheel wells and under the car and check it out. I drove 50 miles once to look at a Mercedes, the ad in the paper said 'no winters'.<P>  Turns out the owner had a newer one, and was using this one as his winter car. (the guy had problems - I walked away from that one).||||reg||1
Z||000004||Oreif||05-15-2001||10:52 AM||||<BR><B><BR>So, my questions are:<BR>For 1000, is this a pretty good deal? </B><P><I>Sounds about right for a low mileage 86 with some problems. You could probably get it him down to $800 if you brought up all that is wrong. Still even a $1000 isn't bad for what you describe.</I>  <P><B>I have my 88GT rims sitting around collecting dust, would these fit on it?</B><P><I> The 88 GT front wheels have a different offset than the back. I have not seen how the change in offset affects handling, but I wouldn't do it. You just need 2 more of the rears (or a pair of 86/87 wheels) to match the offset.</I><P><BR><B>I know the dog bone is shot on the car, so I need to replace that, but should I replace the other mounts as well?</B><P><I> If the dogbone is worn, odds are the others are worn also. It would be better to replace them all up front. It could be part of the shaking/roughness at idle. </I><BR>  <P><B>How hard is it to replace the e-brake cables?</B><P><I>Replacing the cables isn't too hard, but time consuming. This may also cause your phantom chimes. If the E-brake doesn't have tension on it, the handle can bounce slightly on the small switch and cause a ding. </I><P><B>If the headlights are coming up fine, but going down intermittently, is this a sign of the motors going?</B><P><I>The bushings that the motor has on it's gudes are worn. Rodney has a kit to replace these.</I><P><B>The "shudder" that I get, I think it's a bad Torque converter lockup solenoid, is that a pretty good guess?</B><P><I>It could be the problem, I would replace the worn mounts first. Then drive it. If it shudders that reach under and disconnect the connector for the solenoid. If it goes away, replace it. Also if the solenoid went bad or sticks, it could have assisted in wearing out the mounts.</I><P><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||AkursedX||05-15-2001||02:32 PM||||Thanks alot for all the responses guys!   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Most likely, I am going to offer him 800 bucks for it.  As I figure it, minus the cost of tires, I will be putting about 500-600 bucks into it to get it in real good running order. This includes (e-brake cables, motor mounts, ignition tune up, and other miscellanious pieces)  But I sold my other winter car for 3200, so I have alot of cash to play with  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  BTW, this is going to be my winter car.  It has been through some winters, and the underneath has some rust, but I have seen plenty worse.  It's mainly surface rust, and I will have all summer to sandblast and clean everything up.  <P>Oh, how hard is it to change the motor and tranny mounts?  ||||reg||14
Z||000006||Fformula88||05-15-2001||09:14 PM||||Sounds like an excellent find.  Everyone's advice seems sound to me.  Definately do all the engine mounts!  Do do the lower mounts, I would imagine your going to have to support the engine to get the weight off of them.  Then it should be straight forward.  The dog bone is a peice of cake.<P>You could give the 88 front rims a shot and see how it drives/handles.  Make sure there is no rubbing of the tires when doing turns.  There is a lot of extra room in the wheel wells, so I am guessing your going to be ok.  If not, you can always pick up an extra pair of rims.<P>Headlights sound like they are going to need some work.  Either rebuild or replacement of the motors.<P>I have found that the E-brake cables aren't too bad.  Since your replacing them, you don't have to be too kind to them.  It helps to have an extra pair of hands is some spots too.<P>Try talking him down a little, but as long as it doesn't need major engine/tranny work it doesn't sound like a bad deal to me!||||reg||1
